**Goal** — Add per-turn native steering through the session facade and inject pending messages at safe loop boundaries.

**Motivation** — Typing while a turn runs is wasted: `ChatInput.submit` drops a non-slash return while `busy` is true, so a thought has to be held until the turn ends, and there is no way to redirect a long turn short of cancelling it with escape and retyping.

**Approach** — The **facade** owns each session's pending steers, in arrival order:

- `steer(text)` throws `AGENT_SESSION_CLOSED` on a closed session and `AGENT_SESSION_INVALID_OPTIONS` for empty or whitespace-only text. It returns `{ status: "unsupported" }` when the backend is not `steerable`, `{ status: "no_turn" }` when no **turn** claim is active (a running `runCommand` or `compact` is not a turn), and otherwise appends the text and returns `{ status: "accepted", steerId, applied }`.
- For a steerable backend, each turn's `sendTurn` options carry a `SteerChannel` whose `take()` removes every pending steer, resolves each one's `applied` with `true`, and returns their texts in order.
- When the turn claim settles (`onSettle`, which runs before `turn_end` is emitted), the channel closes: every steer still pending resolves `applied` with `false` and is discarded. A claim voided before the turn started settles the same way. Pending steers never carry into the next `send()`.

The native turn loop calls `take()` at two points:

1. **Round boundary.** At the top of every `runRoundTripLoop` iteration after the first, **after** the deadline check and only when the turn signal is not aborted, before `maybeCompact`: the taken texts are appended to `state.messages` as `{ role: "user", content: text }`, in order, after the previous round's tool results. Two consecutive user messages (tool results, then the steer) is intentional: the compaction path already produces that order, and nax-ai passes it to the provider unchanged.
2. **Turn end.** In `runTurnEndPhase`, when the turn is not `stopped` and the signal is not aborted: if `take()` returns texts, they are appended as user messages, followed by a `before_turn_end` `followUp` when the handler returned one within its cap, and the turn continues exactly as a honoured `followUp` does (including `spinFlags.warned = false`). Steers do not count against `MAX_FOLLOW_UPS_PER_TURN`.

Injected steers are ordinary transcript user messages, so they persist and replay. No new `SessionEvent` type is introduced, so the ACP server's event translator is untouched.

Integration targets: the facade's active claim carries a kind (`"turn" | "command" | "compact"`) so `steer` can tell a turn from a command; the turn run adds the facade's `SteerChannel` to the options it passes `adapter.sendTurn` when `opened.steerable`. The cap gates only the `followUp`; pending steers still continue the turn when the cap is reached. Add the native backend's `steerable` flag. Create `src/session/steer-channel.ts` for the facade's pending-steer list, `SteerChannel` and settle-time discard. Export `SteerResult` and `SteerChannel` by name from `@nathapp/nax-agent` `.` and regenerate `api/nax-agent.api.txt` with `bun run api:update`. Preserve compatibility with optional AgentSession methods while the facade always supplies steer.

**Scope** — In: session contracts, per-claim channel lifecycle, native capability, round-boundary/turn-end injection and transcript persistence/replay. Out: manual compaction implementation (US-002), message queue (US-003), host/ctrl+s wiring (US-004). Steering on ACP brains: the ACP protocol has no mid-turn user input, so ctrl+s interrupts and sends instead. No new `SessionEvent` type; the ACP server event translator is not changed.

**Interface**
```ts
export type SteerResult =
  | { readonly status: "accepted"; readonly steerId: string; readonly applied: Promise<boolean> }
  | { readonly status: "unsupported" }
  | { readonly status: "no_turn" };

interface SteerChannel { take(): readonly string[] }
// AgentSession (optional contract; facade always provides it)
steer?(text: string): SteerResult;
// OpenedBackend
readonly steerable?: true;
// SendTurnOpts
steers?: SteerChannel;
```

**Goal** — Expose on-demand native history compaction with single-flight session ownership, cancellation and exact spend reporting.

**Motivation** — Compaction only happens automatically, near the context limit. There is no way to shed a long, finished exploration before starting the next task.

**Approach** — `NativeSessionAdapter.compactHistory(handle, { signal })` loads the stored history, estimates `tokensBefore` with `estimateContextTokens(messages, undefined, undefined)`, plans with `prepareCompaction(messages, keepBudget(window, compaction))`, and when a plan exists calls the exported `summarizeAndApply` with the summarizer from the shared factory, saves the compacted transcript (same owner and model identity as a turn save), and clears the session's stored `lastUsage` anchor so the next turn re-estimates from the compacted array. The summarizer factory takes everything the turn's closure uses — the client for the session's model, its pricing rate card, the resolved context window (`resolveContextWindow`) and the session's compaction settings (`state.compaction.get(id)`, falling back to `DEFAULT_COMPACTION`) — so `sendTurn` and `compactHistory` resolve them the same way. It ignores `compaction.enabled`, which governs automatic compaction only. When `prepareCompaction` returns `undefined` it returns `compacted: false`, `tokensAfter === tokensBefore`, zero usage and zero cost, without calling the summarizer. A summarizer error propagates and the stored history is unchanged. It does not dispatch `before_compaction`.

The facade's `compact()` refuses, in order: `AGENT_SESSION_CLOSED`, `AGENT_SESSION_BUSY` (a turn, command, configure or compact in flight), `AGENT_SESSION_CAPABILITY_UNSUPPORTED` (backend has no `compact`). It holds the session's single slot while it runs (so `send` refuses `AGENT_SESSION_BUSY`) under the session's `turnTimeoutSeconds` deadline, and the signal it passes aborts on that deadline or on `close()`.

Integration targets: `NativeSessionAdapter.sendTurn` builds the `summarize` closure inline from the turn's client, resolved model, rates and signal; history is reloaded from the transcript store at every turn start. The `summarize` closure is built by an extracted factory used by both `sendTurn` and a new `compactHistory(handle, { signal }): Promise<CompactResult>`. `summarizeAndApply` — Target: exported, unchanged behaviour. `OpenedBackend` gains `compact`; the native backend delegates to adapter.compactHistory, while the ACP backend implements neither new capability and is not changed. The facade always provides compact even though the interface method is optional. Use the active claim kind `"compact"` alongside `"turn" | "command"`; coordinate this additive shape with US-001 without introducing a dependency. Create `src/native/session/manual-compaction.ts` for compactHistory's body and `src/native/session/session-summarizer.ts` for the extracted factory. Export `CompactResult` by name from the public entry and regenerate `api/nax-agent.api.txt` using `bun run api:update`.

AC-2 is authoritative: after successful manual compaction the next send request must begin with the compaction summary message, starting with COMPACTION_SUMMARY_PREFIX. Existing applyCompaction pins the first message before a summary; reconcile that manual-path mismatch without changing the automatic-compaction summary request regression required by AC-14.

**Scope** — In: shared summarizer extraction, native compaction capability, transcript persistence and usage-anchor invalidation, facade single-slot/deadline/close behavior. Out: steering injection (US-001), ChatHost accounting and /compact UI (US-005). Manual compaction does not dispatch the `before_compaction` loop event; plugin handlers see only proactive and overflow compactions. `/compact` takes no argument; focus instructions for the summary are not supported.

**Interface**
```ts
export interface CompactResult {
  readonly compacted: boolean;
  readonly tokensBefore: number;
  readonly tokensAfter: number;
  readonly usage: TokenUsage;
  readonly costUsd: number;
}
// AgentSession (optional contract; facade always provides it)
compact?(): Promise<CompactResult>;
// OpenedBackend
compact?(request: { signal: AbortSignal }): Promise<CompactResult>;
// NativeSessionAdapter
compactHistory(handle, { signal }): Promise<CompactResult>;
```

**Goal** — Queue busy plain-message returns and submit one queued message per completed turn through the normal send path.

**Motivation** — Typing while a turn runs is wasted: `ChatInput.submit` drops a non-slash return while `busy` is true, so a thought has to be held until the turn ends, and there is no way to redirect a long turn short of cancelling it with escape and retyping.

**Approach** — Message queue (TUI):

- Return while a turn runs, on a draft that is not a slash line, appends the text to the queue and clears the prompt. A slash line is never queued: it keeps today's behaviour (the `TURN_BUSY_NOTICE` notice, `/quit` excepted).
- Above the input, the queue draws at most `MAX_QUEUED_ROWS` muted rows `queued · <text>` (one line each), plus `+N more` when longer; these rows count against the bottom region's row budget like the working line does.
- When a turn's send settles and the turn ended `completed`, after `keys.finishTurn()` has run, the first queued message is submitted through the same path as a typed return (so `@` mentions expand and it gets its own `user` row, title and history entry). One message per turn, in order.
- When a turn ends any other way (`cancelled`, `errored`, `timed_out`, or the send rejects) and **no interrupt-send is pending**, nothing is sent: the queued texts move back into the prompt via `queueToPrompt`, and the queue empties.
- A draft holding an image placeholder is not queued: the notice `images cannot be queued; send it after the turn ends` is shown and the draft stays.
- Queued messages die with the process.
- While `/compact` runs, the screen is busy exactly as during a turn (`sending` and `busy` held, working line `compacting…`), so return queues; when the compaction settles the queue drains as after a completed turn.

Integration targets: `ChatInput` gets a new optional prop `onQueue?(text: string): boolean`; a busy non-slash return calls it, and clears the draft only when it returns `true`. Without the prop the baseline behaviour stands. Create immutable `src/tui/chat/message-queue.ts`. Wire queue rows and completion-dependent draining in ChatApp, leaving the interrupt-send integration to US-004 and compaction lifecycle to US-005. Replace `test/ui/chat/chat-app.test.tsx`'s "AC-4 keeps draft and ignores Enter during a turn" so a busy non-slash return clears the prompt, draws `queued · draft`, and still does not call `send` during the turn. In `test/ui/chat/chat-input.test.tsx`, busy return retaining the draft applies only without onQueue; with onQueue true the text is received and the draft clears. Track the observed top-level turn_end status rather than assuming any resolved send means completed, and retain mention/image preparation semantics when draining.

**Scope** — In: immutable queue operations, optional busy-input callback, queue display/budget, FIFO normal-send draining and failed-turn prompt restoration. Out: native steering (US-001), ctrl+s/steering rows/interrupt-send ownership (US-004), /compact operation lifecycle (US-005). Queued messages are not persisted across a restart or a session switch, and cannot be edited or reordered in place. A message carrying an image cannot be queued or steered.

**Interface**
```ts
// packages/nax/src/tui/chat/message-queue.ts — immutable
export type MessageQueue = readonly string[];
export function pushQueued(queue: MessageQueue, text: string): MessageQueue;
export function pushQueuedFront(queue: MessageQueue, text: string): MessageQueue;
export function takeQueued(queue: MessageQueue): { readonly text: string; readonly rest: MessageQueue } | undefined;
export function queueToPrompt(queue: MessageQueue, draft: string): string;
export const MAX_QUEUED_ROWS = 3;
// ChatInput optional prop
onQueue?(text: string): boolean;
```

**Goal** — Bind ctrl+s to native steering or prioritized interrupt-send while exposing ChatHost.steer and extracting host contracts.

**Motivation** — Typing while a turn runs is wasted: `ChatInput.submit` drops a non-slash return while `busy` is true, so a thought has to be held until the turn ends, and there is no way to redirect a long turn short of cancelling it with escape and retyping.

**Approach** — `ChatApp`'s own `useInput` owns ctrl+s and reads `ChatApp`'s own `draft.editor.text`. A `host.steer` that throws appends an error row with the message and keeps the draft. With a non-empty, non-slash draft:

| State | Effect |
|:--|:--|
| no turn running | same as return: sends the draft |
| turn running, `host.steer(text)` returns `accepted` | prompt clears; a muted `steering · <text>` row shows above the queue rows; `applied` `true` → the row goes and a transcript `user` row with the text is appended; `applied` `false` → the row goes and the text goes to the **front** of the queue |
| turn running, `unsupported`, `no_turn`, or `host.steer` absent | interrupt-send: the text becomes the pending interrupt-send, the prompt clears, and the turn is cancelled as escape does; when the send settles, the pending text is sent first and the queue stays queued, draining after it |

An empty or slash draft makes ctrl+s do nothing. A draft holding an image is refused with the queue's notice. `/help` appends the line `ctrl+s  steer the running turn (or interrupt and send)` after the built-in lines, on native and ACP brains alike.

`ChatHost.steer` returns `{ status: "no_turn" }` when no session is open, and otherwise the session's `steer` result.

Integration targets: `ChatHost`, `ChatHostDeps`, `ChatHostState`, `ChatSendOptions` — Target: move from `chat-host.ts` into a new `chat/chat-host-types.ts` that `chat-host.ts` re-exports (US-004), freeing `chat-host.ts` (597 lines, 600-line cap) for the wiring below. Create `chat/host-steer-compact.ts` — the host's `steer` (and, from US-005, `compact`), kept out of `chat-host.ts`, which sits at the 600-line source cap. Keep additions from independent US-006 intact if they already landed. `ChatHost.steer` is optional so hand-built test hosts typecheck; the real host delegates through the session facade. Reuse US-003's `pushQueuedFront` for unapplied steers. Since facade onSettle resolves pending applied promises before turn_end emission, preserve this ordering when deciding restoration, queue draining and interrupt-send priority in the screen. Update `test/unit/tui/chat/slash-commands-acp.test.ts`'s exact AC-7 help expectation with the trailing ctrl+s line after built-ins.

**Scope** — In: type extraction/re-exports, host steering delegation, ctrl+s, steering-row lifecycle, front requeue, escape-equivalent cancellation and interrupt-send precedence, help hint. Out: native channel implementation (US-001), basic queue model (US-003), manual compaction host/UI (US-005), recent-model persistence/picker ordering (US-006). Steering on ACP brains: the ACP protocol has no mid-turn user input, so ctrl+s interrupts and sends instead. A message carrying an image cannot be queued or steered.

**Interface**
```ts
// ChatHost
steer?(text: string): SteerResult;
// From @nathapp/nax-agent . (US-001)
export type SteerResult =
  | { readonly status: "accepted"; readonly steerId: string; readonly applied: Promise<boolean> }
  | { readonly status: "unsupported" }
  | { readonly status: "no_turn" };
```
The relocated ChatHost, ChatHostDeps, ChatHostState and ChatSendOptions remain re-exported from chat-host.ts so existing consumers keep their import paths.

**Goal** — Add /compact to native chat with exact host spend accounting, busy lifecycle and queue-aware completion.

**Motivation** — Compaction only happens automatically, near the context limit. There is no way to shed a long, finished exploration before starting the next task.

**Approach** — `/compact` (TUI):

- Built-in: inserted in `COMMANDS` directly after `agents` and before `jobs`, with the `COMMAND_DESCRIPTIONS` entry `compact the conversation history`. While a turn runs it gets the generic `TURN_BUSY_NOTICE` like every built-in.
- Native brain: shows the notice `compacting…`, calls `host.compact()`, then shows `compacted: <before> → <after> tokens` (counts via `formatTokens`) or `nothing to compact`. The footer's context reading becomes `tokensAfter`.
- A host without `compact`: the notice `compaction is not available in this session`.
- ACP brain: `acpDispatch` forwards `compact` to the agent as typed (`/compact<rest>`), as it does non-built-ins.
- `ChatHost.compact` records the spend when `costUsd > 0` or any token count is non-zero: one ledger row through `ChatLedger.record` with a synthesized usage event (`type: "usage"`, the session's `sessionId`, a fresh `turnId`, `round: 0`, `at` = now, empty `metadata`, `inputTokens` / `outputTokens` / `cacheRead` / `cacheWrite` from `usage`, `costUsd`; `previousAt` = the time `compact()` was called), and the session metadata's `costUsd` increased by `costUsd`, written to storage and reflected in the host's current handle. `compacted: false` with zero spend writes nothing.
- A rejection shows the error in the transcript; the session stays usable.

While `/compact` runs, the screen is busy exactly as during a turn (`sending` and `busy` held, working line `compacting…`), so return queues; when the compaction settles the queue drains as after a completed turn.

Integration targets: add optional `ChatHost.compact?(): Promise<CompactResult>` to the types extracted in US-004; implement compact beside steer in US-004's `chat/host-steer-compact.ts` and delegate to the open session's compact method. Follow `chat-ledger.ts`'s record contract and `turn-runner.ts`'s metadata accumulation pattern, including cache token spend and current-handle refresh; do not overwrite accumulated cost using a stale opener's metadata. Thread the result's tokensAfter to the footer without inventing SessionEvents. Ensure compact's busy/sending flags release in finally so a rejection cannot strand input. US-002 supplies the facade's mutual exclusion, deadline and native implementation; this story owns host accounting and the UI. Update `test/ui/chat/slash-menu.test.tsx`'s "AC-10 seventeen matches draw eight rows" overflow row from `… 10 more` to `… 11 more`.

**Scope** — In: host compact delegation and ledger/metadata updates, slash command/menu/help, native compact busy display, context reading, ACP forwarding, queue drain and rejection recovery. Out: native summarizer/claim implementation (US-002), steering/interrupt-send implementation (US-004), recent-model picker (US-006), stats/title modules (US-007/008). Manual compaction does not dispatch the `before_compaction` loop event; plugin handlers see only proactive and overflow compactions. `/compact` takes no argument; focus instructions for the summary are not supported.

**Interface**
```ts
// ChatHost (optional so hand-built test hosts typecheck)
compact?(): Promise<CompactResult>;
// From @nathapp/nax-agent . (US-002)
export interface CompactResult {
  readonly compacted: boolean;
  readonly tokensBefore: number;
  readonly tokensAfter: number;
  readonly usage: TokenUsage;
  readonly costUsd: number;
}
```
`ChatLedger.record(event: ChatUsageEvent, previousAt, model?, role?)` remains the accounting seam.

**Goal** — Persist the five most recently used provider-qualified models with their last effort and prioritize them in /model.

**Motivation** — `/model` lists the catalogue in catalogue order with each model at its configured effort. Switching back to the model and effort used an hour ago means finding it again and cycling the effort again, and two providers can offer the same model name (`openai/gpt-6.1-sol` and `openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol`).

**Approach** — Recent models (`/model`):

- Store: `chat-recent-models.json` in the chat sessions directory, `{ "version": 1, "models": [...] }`, newest first, at most `MAX_RECENT_MODELS`, unique by base (`parseModelSpec(model).model`, i.e. `provider/model`). Recording a model whose base is listed replaces that entry with the new effort and moves it to the front.
- `composeChat` creates the store next to the ACP catalog, loads it once before `createChatHost`, and passes `{ store, initial }` as `ChatHostDeps.recents`. The `@/chat` barrel exports `createRecentModelStore` and `RecentModelStore`.
- `useChatPicker` passes `recents: host.recentModels?.()` to `pickerModel`.
- The host records (and saves) on a successful `start`, `resume`, and every `switchTo` that changes the model or its effort. A failed switch records nothing. A save failure warns once per failure and never fails the switch.
- The model picker lists recent rows first, newest first, then the remaining catalogue rows in catalogue order without the bases already shown. A recent row's effort is its stored effort and its detail reads `recent · effort <level>`; the session's own base is marked `current` wherever it appears, and `modelNote` applies to recent rows as to catalogue rows. A recent whose base is not in the host's models is not shown.
- Load failure (unreadable or wrong version): warn, empty list.

Integration targets: mirror `createAcpOfferStore`: versioned JSON beside the sessions directory, atomic write (temp + rename), load failure warns and returns empty. Missing files are empty without warning. A failed save must clean up its temporary file and propagate to the host's warning boundary, where the successful operation remains successful. Create `chat/recent-models.ts` for the store and `recordRecentModel`; create `chat/host-recents.ts` — the host's recording and `recentModels`, kept out of `chat-host.ts`. `ChatHost.recentModels` and `ChatHostDeps.recents` are optional for compatibility. This story has no dependency on US-004: extend the currently available host type declarations, preserving or using their re-exported location if that extraction has already landed. Record the actual successfully selected effort after clamping and rollback/error checks, not just the requested string; mode-only/no-op switches do not change recents. In `test/unit/cli/chat.test.ts`, the fixture spreads `_chatCommandDeps`; add a stub `createRecentModelStore` so the chat command tests never touch the real global sessions directory.

**Scope** — In: recent-model list/store, composition seam/loading, host lifecycle recording/save warnings, picker ordering and remembered effort. Out: host steer/compact extraction (US-004/005), native steering/compaction APIs (US-001/002), queue/stats/title features (US-003/007/008).

**Interface**
```ts
// packages/nax/src/chat/recent-models.ts
export const RECENT_MODELS_FILE = "chat-recent-models.json";
export const MAX_RECENT_MODELS = 5;
export function recordRecentModel(recents: readonly string[], model: string): readonly string[];
export interface RecentModelStore { load(): Promise<readonly string[]>; save(models: readonly string[]): Promise<void>; }
export function createRecentModelStore(input: { path: string; logger?: Pick<Logger, "warn"> }): RecentModelStore;
// ChatHost
recentModels?(): readonly string[];
// ChatHostDeps
recents?: { readonly store: RecentModelStore; readonly initial: readonly string[] };
// ChatCommandDeps (_chatCommandDeps, cli/chat.ts)
createRecentModelStore(input: { path: string; logger }): RecentModelStore;
// PickerModelInput
recents?: readonly string[];
```

**Goal** — Calculate per-turn decoding throughput from live events and append a muted completed-turn stats row.

**Motivation** — There is no measure of how fast a model is decoding, which matters when choosing between providers of the same model.

**Approach** — `decodeRate` folds one turn's chat-session events (events whose `metadata.subagentTask` is set are ignored, via `isSubagentEvent`): per `round`, the window runs from that round's first `text_delta` or `thinking_delta` to its `usage` event; a `stream_reset` for the round restarts its window. Rate = (sum of `outputTokens` over rounds with a window longer than zero) ÷ (sum of those windows in seconds), rounded to one decimal. When a turn ends `completed` with `outputTokens > 0`, a `turn-stats` transcript row follows the reply: `48.2 tok/s · 1.3k out · 14s` (output is `turn_end.usage.outputTokens` via `formatTokens`; elapsed is the floor of the whole seconds from `turn_start` to `turn_end`). With no measurable window the `tok/s` part is left out (`1.3k out · 14s`). Cancelled and errored turns, replayed history and `runCommand` lines leave no row.

Integration targets: create `src/tui/chat/turn-stats.ts`. `TranscriptEntry` (`transcript-model.ts`) — Target: adds `{ kind: "turn-stats"; text: string }`, laid out as one muted row. `isSubagentEvent` (`chat-status.ts`) — Target: exported, unchanged behaviour. Integrate the new entry in layout's per-kind dispatcher and ChatApp's live `handleEvent`, resetting any retained event list for each send and filtering nested turn_start/turn_end as well as nested deltas/usage. Keep statistics out of history replay and command-event processing; reuse `formatTokens` rather than creating another token formatter. Weight rates by summed window duration rather than averaging round rates, and exclude nonpositive windows from numerator and denominator.

**Scope** — In: pure decodeRate/turnStatsText helpers, shared subagent predicate export, live top-level turn-event fold, stats entry and one-row muted layout. Out: message queue/steering/compact lifecycle (US-003/004/005), recent-model ordering (US-006), terminal title (US-008). No decode-speed row for replayed history or for a subagent's turns. No new `SessionEvent` type; the ACP server event translator is not changed.

**Interface**
```ts
// packages/nax/src/tui/chat/turn-stats.ts
export function decodeRate(events: readonly SessionEvent[]): number | undefined;
export function turnStatsText(input: { rate: number | undefined; outputTokens: number; elapsedMs: number }): string;
// New TranscriptEntry union arm
{ kind: "turn-stats"; text: string }
```

**Goal** — Set a project-specific terminal title with a busy marker and restore the previous title on chat teardown.

**Motivation** — A chat in a background terminal tab gives no sign whether it is still working.

**Approach** — Only on a TTY (the same `isTTY` check `renderChat` applies before enabling mouse reporting). `<project>` is the base name of `props.cwd ?? props.host.repoRoot`, with control characters removed. Glyphs are `props.theme?.glyphs`, falling back to `resolveChatTheme({ env: {}, colorLevel: 0 }).glyphs`. `renderChat` writes, in order: `ENTER_ALT_SCREEN`, render, `ENABLE_MOUSE`, `PUSH_TITLE`, `setTitleSequence(chatTitle(project, false, glyphs))`. It passes `ChatApp` an `onBusyChange` that writes `setTitleSequence(chatTitle(project, busy, glyphs))`; `ChatApp` calls it whenever `turnRunning` changes. `restore` writes `DISABLE_MOUSE`, `LEAVE_ALT_SCREEN`, then `POP_TITLE` — the last only when `PUSH_TITLE` was written (a render that throws first pops nothing).

Integration targets: create `src/tui/chat/terminal-title.ts` for title push/set/pop sequences and pure chatTitle. `ChatGlyphs` (`theme.ts`) — Target: adds `busy: string` — `"✳"` unicode, `"*"` ASCII. `ChatAppProps` (`ChatApp.tsx`) — Target: adds optional `onBusyChange?(busy: boolean): void`. Derive the busy prefix from glyphs, not hardcoded Unicode, and strip controls from the project name before embedding it in OSC. Keep the existing once-only restore/exit cleanup semantics; do not issue title writes from an early callback before title initialization or after restoration. In `test/ui/chat/index.test.tsx`, assertions on the exact sequence renderChat writes on mount and restore gain PUSH_TITLE and the title after ENABLE_MOUSE, and POP_TITLE after LEAVE_ALT_SCREEN, on a TTY only.

**Scope** — In: pure title sequences/text, busy glyphs, optional ChatApp callback, TTY mount/busy/unmount/exit/throw handling and exact write ordering. Out: queue/steering/compact implementation (US-003/004/005), recent-models (US-006), decode-speed row (US-007). The terminal title does not show a separate marker for a pending approval or question.

**Interface**
```ts
// packages/nax/src/tui/chat/terminal-title.ts
export const PUSH_TITLE: string; // "\u001b[22;0t"
export const POP_TITLE: string;  // "\u001b[23;0t"
export function setTitleSequence(text: string): string; // "\u001b]0;" + text + "\u0007"
export function chatTitle(project: string, busy: boolean, glyphs: ChatGlyphs): string;
// ChatAppProps
onBusyChange?(busy: boolean): void;
// ChatGlyphs
busy: string;
```
